[
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm trying to emulate Agda's parametrized module system using macros. I tried the following.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">section</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Foo'\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">macro_rules</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Unknown constant `A✝`</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>However my <code>macro_rules</code> refers to the specific <code>A</code> in that section, hence later <code>Foo'</code> calls don't work. Is there a way to define <code>Foo'</code> to refer to the variable name <code>A</code> so that it also works at later points? This is ofc very hacky and fragile but extremely helpful for me.</p>",
        "id": 576696795,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando Chu",
        "timestamp": 1772463307
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can use <code>$(mkIdent `A)</code>, i.e. <code>​`(Foo $(mkIdent `A) $B)</code> in this example</p>",
        "id": 576701288,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1772464296
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can also make the <code>Foo'</code> macro local and then redefine it, though</p>",
        "id": 576701463,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1772464331
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"873547\">Robin Arnez</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Macros.20referring.20to.20variable.20names.3F/near/576701463\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>You can also make the <code>Foo'</code> macro local and then redefine it, though</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I was doing this but was looking for a better option, the first one works great, thanks!</p>",
        "id": 576702903,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando Chu",
        "timestamp": 1772464647
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can also disable hygiene:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">section</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Foo'\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hygiene</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">false</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">macro_rules</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- works</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 576784997,
        "sender_full_name": "Kenny Lau",
        "timestamp": 1772472470
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Now I'm wondering if it's possible to make a macro that runs that macro with some specified arguments. E.g.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">foo_with</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>should have the effect same as</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">local</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Foo'\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">local</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">macro_rules</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 576915273,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando Chu",
        "timestamp": 1772527002
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why doesn't the macro take that identifier as an argument? What is the use case?  Disabling hygiene is rarely the thing to do.</p>",
        "id": 576958610,
        "sender_full_name": "Tomas Skrivan",
        "timestamp": 1772539493
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can implement <code>#foo_with</code> using <code>IO.Ref</code> where you store the identifier in that global reference and then read it the foo macro. </p>\n<p>However you probably have to turn your macro to an elaborator to have an access to IO monad.</p>",
        "id": 576959189,
        "sender_full_name": "Tomas Skrivan",
        "timestamp": 1772539685
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"346070\">Tomas Skrivan</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Macros.20referring.20to.20variable.20names.3F/near/576958610\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why doesn't the macro take that identifier as an argument? What is the use case?  Disabling hygiene is rarely the thing to do.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The use case is is parametrized modules. I got some type <code>Foo</code> that depends on a bunch of parameters (it's derivations in a specific formalized logic, but that doesn't really matter). However, when working on one specific file I'd like all these parameter to be some fixed (usually abstract) value. Sadly these can't be inferred, hence I'd have to manually provide them. With <code>foo_with</code> I can fix for the rest of the file the parameters of interest.</p>\n<p>Disabling hygiene or not is somewhat orthogonal, <code>foo_with</code> could also be implemented with Robin's <code>mkIdent</code> comment.</p>",
        "id": 576960536,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando Chu",
        "timestamp": 1772540145
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ahh this is similar to my <code>set_default_scalar K</code>. Often when working with vector spaces you have one fixed field over which you do everything so you can write</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Field</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">set_default_scalar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and I have notation <code>X -&gt;L Y</code> which expands into mathlib's <code>X -&gt;L[K] Y</code>.</p>\n<p>Code <a href=\"https://github.com/lecopivo/SciLean/blob/master/SciLean/Analysis/Scalar/Notation.lean\">https://github.com/lecopivo/SciLean/blob/master/SciLean/Analysis/Scalar/Notation.lean</a> it is just a smart use of local macro rules.</p>",
        "id": 576975823,
        "sender_full_name": "Tomas Skrivan",
        "timestamp": 1772544674
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So your macro would look something like</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">local</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">syntax</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Foo'\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">local</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">macro_rules</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">defaultFooParm</span><span class=\"bp\">%</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 576977398,
        "sender_full_name": "Tomas Skrivan",
        "timestamp": 1772545088
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And <code>#foo_with</code> just defines local notation <code>defaultFooParm%</code>.</p>",
        "id": 576978702,
        "sender_full_name": "Tomas Skrivan",
        "timestamp": 1772545397
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This seems to be what I was looking for, thanks!</p>",
        "id": 576982723,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando Chu",
        "timestamp": 1772546354
    }
]